<ns0:para xmlns:ns0="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:ns1="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" id="fs-id1167836315013">It is very convenient to name a function and most often we name it <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">f</ns0:emphasis>, <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">g</ns0:emphasis>, <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">h</ns0:emphasis>, <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">F</ns0:emphasis>, <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">G</ns0:emphasis>, or <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">H</ns0:emphasis>. In any function, for each <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">x</ns0:emphasis>-value from the domain we get a corresponding <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">y</ns0:emphasis>-value in the range. For the function <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">f</ns0:emphasis>, we write this range value <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">y</ns0:emphasis> as <ns1:math><ns1:mrow><ns1:mi>f</ns1:mi><ns1:mrow><ns1:mo>(</ns1:mo><ns1:mi>x</ns1:mi><ns1:mo>)</ns1:mo></ns1:mrow><ns1:mo>.</ns1:mo></ns1:mrow></ns1:math> This is called function notation and is read <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">f</ns0:emphasis> of <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">x</ns0:emphasis> or the value of <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">f</ns0:emphasis> at <ns0:emphasis effect="italics">x</ns0:emphasis>. In this case the parentheses does not indicate multiplication.</ns0:para>
